---
/*
 * Shipping & Returns.
 *
 * Linked from the footer since the footer existed, and a 404 until now — which
 * is the worst place to have a gap, because this is the page somebody opens
 * with their card already out, or after a parcel is late.
 *
 * Every number on it is read from lib/orders.ts and lib/shop.ts rather than
 * typed into the prose. The free-shipping threshold quoted here is therefore
 * the same constant the checkout charges against, and cannot drift from it.
 */
import PolicyPage from '../components/PolicyPage.astro';
import { FREE_SHIPPING_OVER, SHIPPING_FLAT } from '../lib/orders';
import {
  DELIVERY_DAYS,
  DISPATCH_DAYS,
  PAYMENT_WINDOW_MINUTES,
  RETURNS_WINDOW_DAYS,
  SHOP_EMAIL,
} from '../lib/shop';
import { CARRIERS } from '../lib/fulfilment';

const flat = `$${SHIPPING_FLAT.toFixed(2)}`;
const free = `$${FREE_SHIPPING_OVER.toFixed(2)}`;
const payWindowHours = Math.round(PAYMENT_WINDOW_MINUTES / 60);

/* The couriers that actually collect from the workshop, minus the two non-couriers. */
const couriers = CARRIERS.filter((c) => c.track).map((c) => c.label);
---

<PolicyPage
  title="Shipping & Returns"
  eyebrow="Getting it to you"
  description="What shipping costs, how long it takes, how to track it, and what happens if something is not right."
  intro="Everything is made and packed by one person in Binh Loi Trung. Here is exactly what that means for how long you wait and what happens if something goes wrong."
  updated="2026-08-28"
>
  <h2>What shipping costs</h2>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>{flat} flat, anywhere.</strong> Free on orders over {free}. The basket works this out
      before you pay, so the total you see at checkout is the total.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    There is one shipping price rather than a table of zones. It is simpler to quote and it is
    honest about what this is: a small workshop posting parcels, not a fulfilment operation with
    negotiated carrier rates. On far-away orders it costs me more than {flat} and I absorb the
    difference.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it takes</h2>

  <p>
    Two separate waits, and it helps to think of them separately — the time it takes me to make and
    pack your order, and the time the courier then has it.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Stage</th>
        <th>How long</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Packed and handed over</td>
        <td>{DISPATCH_DAYS.min}–{DISPATCH_DAYS.max} working days</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>In transit — within Vietnam</td>
        <td>{DELIVERY_DAYS.vietnam.min}–{DELIVERY_DAYS.vietnam.max} working days</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>In transit — everywhere else</td>
        <td>{DELIVERY_DAYS.international.min}–{DELIVERY_DAYS.international.max} working days</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    These are the honest usual, not a guarantee. Tết, a bad storm and the fortnight before Christmas
    all stretch them, and a customs desk can hold an international parcel for as long as it likes.
    If yours is drifting past the top of that range, <a href={`mailto:${SHOP_EMAIL}`}>tell me</a> —
    I would far rather chase it than have you wondering.
  </p>

  <h3>Bundles take the longest</h3>

  <p>
    A bundle is several things gathered into one box, and it does not go out until every part of it
    is in. If one component is on its way to me, that is what your order is waiting on.
  </p>

  <h2>Following your parcel</h2>

  <p>
    Every order gets a number that looks like <strong>ZZ-7K3P9M</strong>. Pair it with the email
    address you ordered under at <a href="/orders/track">order tracking</a> and you will see where
    it has got to — including the courier's own consignment number and a link into their tracking,
    once it has one.
  </p>

  <p>
    That pair is deliberately the whole key: no account, no password. It also means the order number
    on its own opens nothing, which is why I can safely print it on the packing slip.
  </p>

  <p>Parcels go out with whichever of these makes sense for the destination:</p>

  <ul>
    {couriers.map((name) => <li>{name}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    Orders inside Ho Chi Minh City are sometimes brought round in person instead. Those have no
    tracking number because there is no courier — the status page will say delivered when it is.
  </p>

  <h2>Paying</h2>

  <p>
    Checkout hands you to payOS, where you scan a VietQR code with any Vietnamese banking app. No
    card details pass through this site because none are ever asked for.
  </p>

  <p>
    The catalogue is priced in US dollars and the transfer settles in Vietnamese dong, so the
    checkout shows you both figures before you commit. The rate used is a fixed one I set, not a
    live market feed — you are never quoted one number and charged another because a rate moved
    while you were deciding.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>A payment link lasts {payWindowHours} hours.</strong> Nothing is reserved until the
      transfer lands, so an unpaid order does not hold stock. If the window closes, the order is
      cancelled and you are free to start again — nothing was taken.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>If something is not right</h2>

  <p>
    Tell me within <strong>{RETURNS_WINDOW_DAYS} days</strong> of the parcel arriving. Email{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SHOP_EMAIL}`}>{SHOP_EMAIL}</a> with your order number and, if it is a damage
    or a wrong-item problem, a photograph. A photo usually settles it in one message rather than
    three.
  </p>

  <h3>What I will always put right</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The wrong thing arrived.</li>
    <li>It arrived damaged, or damaged in transit.</li>
    <li>It never arrived at all.</li>
    <li>It is not what the listing described.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In those cases you get a replacement or a full refund including the shipping you paid, whichever
    you prefer, and you will not be asked to post anything back at your own cost.
  </p>

  <h3>Changed your mind</h3>

  <p>
    Unused items in their original condition can come back within the same{' '}
    {RETURNS_WINDOW_DAYS} days for a refund of what you paid for the goods. Return postage is yours
    in that case, and the original shipping is not refunded — it was genuinely spent getting the
    parcel to you.
  </p>

  <h3>What cannot come back</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Yarn that has been wound, cut or worked with.</strong> Once a skein is wound it cannot
      be sold again, and that is usually the first thing that happens to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Made-to-order and customised pieces</strong>, unless the fault is mine.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Digital patterns</strong>, which cannot be un-downloaded.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How a refund reaches you</h2>

  <p>
    By bank transfer, back to you, by hand. payOS does not reverse a VietQR payment, so there is no
    button either of us can press — I send the money back the same way it came. Expect it within a
    few working days of us agreeing the refund, and I will give you the transfer reference so you
    can find it on your statement.
  </p>

  <h2>Cancelling</h2>

  <p>
    If your order has not been packed yet, email me and I will cancel it and refund you in full.
    Once it has been handed to a courier it has to run its course — at that point it becomes a
    return rather than a cancellation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Handmade things vary a little: colours shift between dye lots and between screens, and no two
    pieces come out identical. That is the nature of the thing rather than a fault — but if what
    arrived is not what you thought you were buying, say so and we will sort it out.
  </p>
</PolicyPage>
